# Process 9, the body of source.NewReloader: it puts the first snapshot in
# service, and then, every refresh interval, runs boxes 9.2, 9.3 and 9.1
# again in reloader.run, while box 9.4 answers each request.
# By hand: dfd --box 300x150 --per-row 5 --number --number-prefix 9. docs/flow.9.dfd -o flow.9.svg

{3 Build the portal handler}
> listing, portal handler
[9.1. Put the snapshot's listing and portal handler in service
 so that every request from now on reads that snapshot
 (source.reloader.swap)]
    > snapshot in service
    |Reloader|
    > swap
    <Log>
>
[9.2. Check the source: list it, and compare the listing with the snapshot in service and with the last check's
 to find a settled change
 (source.reloader.check)]
    < snapshot in service, last listing
    > last listing
    |Reloader|
    > keys
    < listing
    <Bucket folder>
    > problem
    <Log>
> settled listing
[9.3. Read the settled listing's snapshot and build its portal handler with the builder
 so that the new snapshot replaces the old one in one step
 (source.reloader.rebuild)]
    > keys
    < files
    <Bucket folder>
    > problem
    <Log>
> listing, portal handler
[9.1. Put the snapshot's listing and portal handler in service
 so that every request from now on reads that snapshot
 (source.reloader.swap)]
    > snapshot in service
    |Reloader|
    > swap
    <Log>
> response writer, request
[9.4. Answer each request with the portal handler of the snapshot in service
 (source.reloader.ServeHTTP)]
    < snapshot in service
    |Reloader|
    > response
    <HTTP server>

# type: listing = source.Listing
# type: portal handler = net/http.Handler
# type: keys = []string
# type: settled listing = source.Listing
# type: files = [][]byte
# type: problem = error
# type: swap = string
# type: response writer = net/http.ResponseWriter
# type: request = *net/http.Request
# type: response = []byte
